Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (AC32, AC33, AC34, AC53)
Research: Q2 (block types), Q5 (tool_call_id from tool_use_id), Q8 (tool_order and server_tool assembled from recorded blocks)
Why this approach: the mapping (tool_use client; server_tool_use, mcp_tool_use, mcp_tool_result and *_tool_result with tool_use_id provider) already landed in T20/T22, so this task adds the fixtures and the three tests, each checking both the parser's events and the canonicalized stored payloads. tool_turn's recorded answer is a lone tool_use, so tool_order's text blocks are websearch.sse's recorded text blocks 3 and 7, in tool_turn's envelope; server_tool keeps websearch.sse's envelope. Each block event is byte-identical to its recording except its top-level "index", renumbered 0, 1, 2 because the stream parser orders blocks by index (checked by a throwaway script; recorded in README and plan.md "Details fixed in T23").
Alternatives rejected: keeping the recorded index values (tool_turn's tool_use is index 0, so text before it is impossible, and it collides with server_tool_use's 0); tool_turn's message_delta for server_tool (mixes two recordings' usage).
Trade-off / known limit: server_tool's stop_reason stays end_turn as recorded although the turn ends in a client tool_use; the parser does not read it for tool mapping.
Verified by: TestParse_ToolUseTurn, TestParse_MessageKeepsToolBlockOrder, TestParse_ServerToolUse (passed on arrival; proved by temporarily mapping server tools to client, prefixing tool_call_id and reversing block order, each failing the tests, then reverting); TestFixtures_NoIdentifiers over the new files; make verify.
